erpretation**: the spike eigenvalues are determined by the second-moment of pi(N; q, a) over residue classes (Gallagher 1970, Montgomery-Vaughan MNT I Ch.17), so the C2 spectral barrier reduces to the Gallagher 2nd-moment — a quantity itself controlled by Bombieri-Vinogradov/PNT-in-AP, with no polylog method. (d) Closes the "look-up L(1, chi) to compute pi(N) via spectral compression" route hoped for by S82. **Closure mode C** (circular): to compute `sum_block sigma^2` one would need pi(N; p, *) for small p, which is itself the PNT-in-AP problem. Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82. Cross-domain refs: Gallagher 1970 *Mathematika 23*; Montgomery-Vaughan 2007 *MNT I* Ch.17; Davenport 2000 *MNT* Ch.6 (closed-form L(1, chi) via digamma). See experiments/constructions/spike_eigenvalue_l_squared/. | 148 |
    /apps/aplikacijos/prime-research/status/CLOSED_PATHS.md:788:| §D27 ATTACK_VECTORS — Newman / Erdős L^∞-flatness of χ_P-coefficient polynomial f_N(z) = Σ_{n≤N} χ_P(n) z^n: does R_N := ‖f_N‖_∞ / √π(N) saturate Erdős/Newman flat-polynomial bound? (cross-domain: Newman 1976 Proc. AMS 51 / Erdős 1957 Mich. Math. J. 4 / Salem-Zygmund / Bourgain Λ(p) — Kahane 1985 CUP §6) | FAIL | I | Frontier wild_swing S138 on the L^∞ endpoint (p=∞) of the Fourier-side characterisation of χ_P, complementary to D10/E2.20 (Mahler = log integral) and D25 (Stein-Tomas L^p, finite p). FFT-based ‖f_N‖_∞ = max_k |F[k]| via M = 16(N+1) oversampled DFT; major-arc decomposition exposes |F(e^{2πi a/q})|/√π(N) =: R(a/q) at every rational. Ensembles: 50 matched-density Bernoulli (uniform support), 50 matched-parity Bernoulli (odd support, single p=2 included), 50 signed-prime Littlewood, deterministic Liouville f_λ, Rudin-Shapiro at length π(N). Run at N ∈ {2^{10}, 2^{12}, 2^{14}, 2^{16}, 2^{18}}. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: (a) |R_N(prime) − R_B| ≤ 0.1 → B-grade Salem-Zygmund typical; (b) R_N(prime) → c < √2 → A-grade super-RudinShapiro flat; (c) R_N(prime) >> R_B → I-mode HL singular-series imprint. **Outcome: B-grade case (c) mode I — HL singular series is recovered EXACTLY at every rational point.** **Empirical identity (verified at all 5 N)**: R(a/q; primes) = √π(N) · μ(q)² / φ(q) + O(√(2 log N)) for every (a, q) = 1 with 1 ≤ q ≤ 64. Squarefree q: relative error 0.1–6% (median 1.5%) across 64 q values at N=2^{18}; non-squarefree q (μ(q)²=0): R ≈ 0.05–0.7 (vanishes within Salem-Zygmund noise). At N=2^{18} (π(N)=23000, √π(N)=151.66): R(q=1)=151.66 (HL=151.66 exact); R(q=2)=151.64 (HL=151.66, 99.99% match — parity peak); R(q=3)={75.60, 75.76} (HL=75.83); R(q=4)=0.36 (μ²=0); R(q=5)={37.71-38.10} (HL=37.91); R(q=8)=0.23 (μ²=0); ... full table in run_full.log covers q ≤ 64. **Bare ‖f_N‖_∞ = (1+o(1)) · √π(N)**, attained at z = -1 across all N, confirmed at both N=2^{16} and 2^{18}. Q_max scan at N=2^{18}: R(prime, minor) = {151.64, 75.76, 75.76, 38.09, 19.17, 12.83, 12.31} for Q_max = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}; R(Bern, minor) ≈ 10.7 ± 0.6 across all Q_max. Z-scores progress {214, 80, 114, 53, 11, 3.1, 3.1}σ — primes converge to Bernoulli noise floor as Q_max → ∞, with persistent ~3σ residual reflecting cumulative O(Q²) HL peaks at q > Q_max. Scaling rule R(prime, minor; Q_max) ≈ √π(N) / φ(q*) where q* = smallest squarefree integer > Q_max verified to ~10% across all (N, Q_max) cells. **Mechanism for closure mode I (partial closure with HL fingerprint as new content)**: the L^∞ structure decomposes EXACTLY into the Hardy-Littlewood circle-method singular series. Major-arc R(a/q) = √π(N)·μ²(q)/φ(q) is the exponential-sum identity Σ_{p≤N} e^{2πi p a/q} ≈ μ(q)/φ(q)·π(N) (classical Vinogradov / Hardy-Littlewood); the bare ‖f‖_∞ is dominated by q=2 parity. **Erdős-extremal flatness DECISIVELY REFUTED**: R_N → ∞ as √π(N), the OPPOSITE extreme; primes are one of the LEAST flat 0/1 supports possible (saturating the trivial upper bound from above via parity). **Distinct from**: D10/E2.20 (Mahler = log integral, geometric mean) — D27 makes the parity major-arc contribution to the Jensen integral explicit at z=-1; D25 (Stein-Tomas L^p, finite p) — D27 covers the p=∞ endpoint; CLOSED_PATHS line 778/E7.16 (Friedman/Ramanujan prime-Cayley spectral gap) — that uses the prime SET as Cayley generators on Z/NZ, this uses χ_P as polynomial coefficients. **Adds EDGE E2.21**: R(a/q; primes) = √π(N)·μ²(q)/φ(q) + O(√log N) at every rational; ‖f_N‖_∞ = (1+o(1))√π(N) attained at z=-1; provides the L^∞-norm endpoint of the L^p Fourier-side category. **Negative-shape consequence**: any L^∞-Chebyshev-style compression of f_N cannot beat √π(N) storage — parity peak forces this lower bound; rules out "flat-polynomial" representation of χ_P as polylog primality witness. **Cross-domain ingredient (Newman / Erdős / Littlewood / Bourgain Λ(p) extremal-polynomial L^∞ harmonic analysis) performed real work**: first project quantitative use; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §2 row "Newman / Erdős polynomial flatness" promoted PROPOSED (D27) → USED I with edge E2.21. **Channelled mathematician**: Vinogradov (major-arc / minor-arc decomposition for prime exponential sums; HL circle method). **Successors proposed (S138)**: D27.a Vinogradov minor-arc supremum at N=2^{18}, 10⁴ random rationals a/q with q ∈ (N^{2/5}, N^{3/5}); does empirical max R(a/q) match `C·N^{1/4}(log N)^A` Vinogradov bound? D27.b Liouville L^∞ at major arcs — Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality predicts R^λ(a/q) = o(1) for ALL q including q=2, screening data already shows ‖f_λ‖_∞/√N = 3.16 with argmax NOT at q=2; quantitative single-session test. D27.c twin-prime Newman polynomial L^∞ uses HL twin-prime singular series ∝ ∏(p-2)/(p-1) — different q-pattern. Cites E1.5, E1.10, E2.13, E2.20. Cross-domain refs: Erdős 1957 *Mich. Math. J.* 4; Newman 1976 *Proc. AMS* 51; Kahane 1985 *Some Random Series of Functions* CUP §6 (Salem-Zygmund); Bourgain 1988 *Acta Math.* 161 (Λ(p) sets); Balister-Bollobás-Morris-Sahasrabudhe 2020 *Annals* 192, 977 (resolution of Erdős for ±1). See experiments/analytic/newman_linfty_chi_p/. | 138 |
    /apps/aplikacijos/prime-research/status/CLOSED_PATHS.md:800:| S166 commit-thread step 2 — analytic theorem for V_p ⊕ V_{2p}^prim energy of chi_P MPS unfolding (replaces S148's empirical "K = 3.86" with an exact Ramanujan-sum formula) [B-grade per S167 verification, demoted from A; theorem itself confirmed but proof tools are textbook NT] | THEOREM+REFINEMENT | C | Commit-thread session 2/5 on the S82 invariant-subspace theorem. **Theorem (S166).** For W=2, `N = 2^d`, the L² energy of `chi_P` in the additive-Fourier subspace `V_p^prim ⊕ V_{2p}^prim` equals `E(p, N) = (2 (pi(N) − O(1))²) / ((p−1) N) + O(p · Var(p, N) / N)` where `Var(p, N) = sum_{a coprime to p} (pi(N; p, a) − pi(N)/(p−1))²` is the Gallagher PNT-in-AP variance. Equivalently in S148's K-form: `K(p, N) = E(p, N) · p · log²(N) / N → 2p/(p−1)` as `N → ∞`, with finite-N correction `(pi(N) log N / N)²`. **Proof sketch**: For each k coprime to q, the additive-Fourier coefficient `S_q^k = sum_{p' prime ≤ N} ω_q^{k p'}` decomposes via Dirichlet character orthogonality as a *principal-character main term* (proportional to the Ramanujan sum `c_q(k)`) plus *non-principal Dirichlet sums* `Psi(N, chi)`. Ramanujan sums: `c_p(k) = mu(p) = -1` (k coprime to p prime), `c_{2p}(k) = mu(2p) = +1` (k coprime to 2p, p odd). Both give `|S_q^k|² ≈ (pi(N) − O(1))²/(p−1)²`. Sum over `phi(q) = p-1` coprime k-values per q ∈ {p, 2p}, divide by N. The non-principal sum is bounded by Plancherel: `≤ p · Var(p, N) / N`. **Empirical verification at 25 cells `(d, p) ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20, 22} × {3, 5, 7, 11, 13}`: ratio empirical/predicted ∈ [0.992, 1.000], mean 0.998, max deviation 0.8%, shrinking as N grows**. Specifically at d=22 (N = 4M, pi(N) = 295,947): pred V_p+V_{2p} = 20881.80, empirical = 20881.27 (ratio 1.0000) for p=3; ratio 1.0000 for p=5, 7; ratio 0.9999 for p=11, 13. **Net new content**: (a) **The "K = 3.86" universal constant from S148 is corrected**: K is *p-dependent*, with `K_∞(p) = 2p/(p-1)` (3.0 / 2.5 / 7/3 / 2.2 for p = 3, 5, 7, 11). The S148 average ≈ 3.86 reflected (i) p=3 weight in the data, (ii) finite-N correction `(pi(N) log N / N)² ≈ 1.18 at d=20`, and (iii) SVD spike-block leakage outside V_p + V_{2p}^prim. (b) **S148's Gallagher-variance framing is REFUTED for the main term**: the main term is a **Ramanujan-sum / principal-character-of-Dirichlet quantity**, NOT a 2nd-moment of primes. The Gallagher variance enters only in the sub-leading remainder. These are different objects governed by different machinery (Ramanujan-sum c_q(k) = mu(q) — deterministic; Gallagher variance — Bombieri-Vinogradov 2nd-moment). (c) **SVD spike-block leakage diagnosed**: empirical `sum sigma²_top-spikes` exceeds `E(p, N)` by 4-38% at d=20 (p=3 / 5 / 7), shrinking with N (e.g., p=3: 18% at d=14 → 4% at d=20); asymptotically the SVD spike block converges to the V_p ⊕ V_{2p}^prim subspace energy. (d) **Sharper structural identification**: chi_P's MPS-unfolding spike content at conductor 2p is **EXACTLY** the principal-Dirichlet-character contribution to mod-p / mod-2p prime distribution, with all character L-function content sitting in the sub-leading remainder. This is sharper than S82's "approximate eigenvector ~ |L(1, chi)|²" claim (refuted in S148) AND sharper than S148's "Gallagher variance" claim. **Closure mode C** (circular): the theorem proves what the spike-block energy IS, but computing E(p, N) requires pi(N) and the remainder requires Gallagher 2nd-moment data. The C-circular collapse stands; what's new is the *exactness* of the analytic identification of the main term. Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82, S148. Cross-domain refs: Hardy-Wright (2008) *An Intro to the Theory of Numbers* Ch. 16 (Ramanujan sums); Iwaniec-Kowalski (2004) *Analytic Number Theory* Ch. 3 (Dirichlet character orthogonality, Gauss sums); Davenport (2000) *MNT* Ch. 6, 8 (PNT-in-AP); Gallagher (1970) *Mathematika* 23 (used only for remainder bound); Montgomery-Vaughan (2007) *MNT I* Ch. 9 (Gauss sums), Ch. 17 (Var of primes in AP). See experiments/constructions/spike_gallagher_proof/. | 166 |
    /apps/aplikacijos/prime-research/status/CLOSED_PATHS.md:802:| S168 commit-thread step 3 — squarefree extension of S166: V_q^prim energy theorem for ALL squarefree q (closes the W=2 chi_P MPS spike-block decomposition) | THEOREM+EXTENSION | C | Commit-thread session 3/5 on the S82 invariant-subspace theorem. **Theorem (S168).** For every squarefree q ≥ 2, with `chi_P` the prime indicator on `{1,..,N}`, `r(q) = #{primes dividing q}`: `E(q, N) = ‖P_{V_q^prim} chi_P‖²/N = μ(q)² · (π(N) − r(q))² / (φ(q) N) + R(q, N)`, with `|R(q, N)| ≤ q · Var(q, N) / N + O(r²/N)`. **Corollary (μ-vanishing)**: for non-squarefree q (μ(q) = 0), the main term vanishes and `E(q, N) = O(q · Var(q, N) / N)`. **Corollary (additivity)**: total chi_P energy in `⊕_{q sqf, q ≤ Q*} V_q^prim` is `(π(N)²/N) · Σ_{q sqf ≤ Q*} 1/φ(q) ~ A · π(N)² log Q* / N` with `A → 1` (Selberg-Delange). **Proof**: same skeleton as S166. For squarefree q, the Ramanujan sum `c_q(k) = μ(q)` (k coprime q) gives the principal-character main term. Non-principal Dirichlet contributions split: induced characters mod q have `c_q^χ(k) = 0`, primitive characters give Gauss sums `|τ(χ)| = √q` and contribute `≤ q · Var(q, N)/N` by Plancherel on (Z/qZ)*. The novelty over S166: closed form `μ(q)²/φ(q)` is uniform across squarefree q irrespective of how many prime factors q has (S166 only proved q ∈ {p, 2p}). **Empirical verification**: 30 squarefree q ∈ [2, 50] × 4 d ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20}, ratios ∈ [0.9912, 1.1652] at d=14 → [0.9998, 1.0017] at d=20; aggregate sum-over-Q* matches predicted to better than 0.3% across Q* ∈ {10, 20, 50, 100, 200} at d=20. **Non-squarefree falsifier passes**: at d=18 for q ∈ {4, 8, 9, 12, 16, 18, 20, 24, 25, 27, ...}, E_emp ≤ q·Var/N strictly, with ratio E_emp/(q Var/N) ≤ 1.0003 (at q=4, where unique non-principal char is primitive; ratio < 1 elsewhere). **Net new content**: (a) closed form for E(q, N) for ALL squarefree q (including composites), not just q ∈ {p, 2p}. (b) Predicts and verifies main-term VANISHING for non-squarefree q. (c) Identifies Wirsing-type sum `Σ_{sqf q ≤ Q} 1/φ(q) ~ log Q · A_∞` with `A_∞ = 1` by Selberg-Delange. (d) **Predicts chi_P spike-block fraction = 0.21 of `‖chi_P‖² = π(N)`** when paired with S74's k_*(N) ~ N^{0.42}: setting `Σ_{sqf q ≤ Q*} φ(q) ~ (3/π²)Q*² = N^{0.42}` gives `Q* ~ N^{0.21}`, hence total spike energy ≈ 0.21 · π(N). The remaining 0.79 · π(N) is bulk Marchenko-Pastur (S74). **Closure mode C**: computing E(q, N) requires π(N; q, a) data which is the PNT-in-AP problem itself; no polylog method opens. What's NEW is a **complete arithmetic decomposition** of `‖chi_P‖²` into named pieces (wheel mode + V_q^prim spike for sqf q + non-sqf-only-variance + bulk MP). Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82, S148, S166. Cross-domain refs: Hardy-Wright Ch.16 (Ramanujan sums); Iwaniec-Kowalski §3.4 (Gauss sums, primitive vs induced); Davenport Ch.6,22 (Ψ(N, χ) bounds); Tenenbaum §I.4.4-5 (Selberg-Delange A=1); Bombieri-Vinogradov 1965/1966 (Var(q,N) range). See experiments/constructions/s166_squarefree_extension/. | 168 |
    /apps/aplikacijos/prime-research/status/CLOSED_PATHS.md:804:| S169 commit-thread step 4 — empirical confirmation of S168's 21% spike-block-fraction prediction + finite-N Q* exponent correction (0.21 → 0.185) | EMPIRICAL+REFINEMENT | C | Commit-thread session 4/5 on the S82 invariant-subspace theorem. **S168 prediction**: total chi_P energy in `⊕_{q sqf, q ≤ Q*} V_q^prim` ≈ 0.21 · π(N), with `Q* ~ N^{0.21}` derived from `Σ_{sqf q ≤ Q*} φ(q) ~ k_*(N) ~ N^{0.42}` (Mertens-Wirsing). **Three pre-stated falsifiers tested at d ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24}** via direct Fourier sieve `E(q, N) = (1/N) Σ_{k coprime q} |Σ_{p' prime ≤ N} ω_q^{k p'}|²` for all squarefree q ∈ [2, ⌈N^{0.30}⌉], plus comparison to SVD spike-block sums from S82 (`spike_d{14,18,20}_results.json`). **Outcomes**: **PR1 (analytic at Q* = N^{0.21})** — PARTIAL. cum(Q*) / (0.21 π(N)) = 1.330, 1.266, 1.260, 1.193, 1.172, 1.167 across d=14..24, monotonically converging to 1 but still 17% above at d=24. The asymptotic Wirsing-A → 1 holds; convergence is slow (order 1/log N). **PR2 (SVD spike block / 0.21·π(N))** — PASS. Ratio 1.0647, 1.0533, 1.0466 at d=14, 18, 20 — within 7% even at d=14, monotonically decreasing toward 1. **PR3 (Q_eff exponent = 0.21 ± 0.05)** — CORRECTED. The matching Q_eff (where analytic cum(Q) equals the SVD spike block) has log Q_eff / log N = 0.1846, 0.1846, 0.1850 at d=14, 18, 20. Stable at exponent **0.185, NOT 0.21** at finite N. **Net new content**: (a) **The 21% fraction prediction holds empirically** — the SVD spike block fraction π_spike / π(N) = 0.224, 0.221, 0.220 at d=14,18,20, within 7% at d=14, decreasing to within 5% at d=20 (S176/S178 scope-correction; original "within 5% at d=14" framing was off by 1.5pp; d=16 added by S173 lands at 0.2132 and breaks monotonicity in d). This validates S168's structural claim that chi_P MPS energy splits ~21%/79% spike/bulk. (b) **The Q* exponent is corrected from 0.21 to 0.185 at finite N**. The S168 derivation `Q* = N^{0.21}` from `k_*(N) = N^{0.42}` has finite-N corrections from both `k_*(N)`'s slowly-varying prefactor and Mertens-Wirsing remainder. The asymptotic `Q* → N^{0.21}` is consistent with the analytic-side trajectory (crossing-Q exponent drifts 0.16 → 0.18 across d=18..24), but at finite N the SVD-matching Q_eff is at exponent 0.185. (c) **"Negative-leakage / missing-spike" effect identified**. The SVD spike block is *smaller* than analytic cum(Q=N^{0.21}) by 12-20% at d=14..20 — opposite sign from S166's "leakage" finding (which was relative to a single E(p, N), not the full cum(Q*)). The reconciliation: S166's "leakage" reflected SVD spikes capturing more than a single V_p^prim sector; S169's "missing-spike" reflects partially-saturated V_q^prim sectors at q > Q_eff whose characters' modes haven't yet emerged from the bulk noise floor at finite d. The actual SVD spike block sits between `E(p, N)` for a single p and the analytic cum(Q*=N^{0.21}). **Closure mode C**: the 21% fraction is an asymptotic structural fact; its computation requires `π(N; q, a)` for q in a small but non-trivial window, and the bulk 79% is GUE-pseudorandom per S74. No polylog opening. What's NEW is empirical confirmation of S168's prediction and a sharp finite-N Q* exponent (0.185). Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82, S148, S166, S168. Cross-domain refs: Hardy-Wright Ch.18 (Mertens); Tenenbaum §I.4.4-5 (Selberg-Delange A=1); Wirsing 1956 *Arch. Math.* 7. See experiments/constructions/spike_block_21pct_test/. | 169 |
    /apps/aplikacijos/prime-research/status/CLOSED_PATHS.md:806:| S190 commit-thread step 5 (FINAL) — synthesis + thread DONE: chi_P MPS spectrum compiles into `π(N) = 0.21·π(N) [PNT-in-AP at sqf q ≤ N^{0.185}] + 0.79·π(N) [GUE-bulk MP]`, sharper closure of polylog spectral attack family | SYNTHESIS+CLOSURE | C | Commit-thread session 5/5 (final synthesis slot) on the S82 invariant-subspace theorem. **Compiled theorem (S190)**: combining S148 (refutation of S82's |L(1,χ)|² eigenvalue claim + Gallagher-variance K-formula), S166 (exact V_p ⊕ V_{2p}^prim energy via Ramanujan sums), S168 (squarefree extension to all sqf q with closed form `μ²(q)/φ(q)·(π(N)−r)²/N + R`), and S169 (empirical 21% spike-block-fraction at d=14,18,20 within 7%/5%/5%, finite-N Q_eff exponent ≈ 0.185), the chi_P MPS column-Gram operator decomposes additively into: (1) rank-1 wheel mean (excluded), (2) spike sector ≅ ⊕_{sqf q ≤ Q*≈N^{0.185}} V_q^prim carrying ≈ 0.21·π(N) energy, (3) bulk MP sector with σ-edge 2√(M·p·(1−p)) (M=√N, p=π(N)/N) carrying ≈ 0.79·π(N) energy. The spike eigenvectors ARE Dirichlet character vectors (S82 stands); the eigenvalues are Ramanujan-sum / principal-Dirichlet structure (NOT |L(1,χ)|², S148/S166). **Polylog-blocker reformulation (NEW for S190)**: computing π(N) ≡ computing the bulk MP sector at additive resolution q ≥ N^{0.185}; the spike sector reduces to PNT-in-AP at sqf q ≤ N^{0.185} computable in O(N^{0.185+ε}) by direct Fourier sieving but NOT polylog (E1.5 anchor); the bulk sector is GUE-pseudorandom across 35+ measures (S74/E1.5/T6 anchor). Both sub-sectors blocked. **Net effect**: structural progress, no algorithmic opening. The closure of the chi_P spectral attack family is now FACTORED into two separately-quantified sub-barriers at separate scales (21% at q≤N^{0.185} subpolynomial / 79% at q≥N^{0.185} information-theoretic), whereas before the closure was monolithic via "E2.1 + S74 → C-circular C2 collapse". **Closure mode C** (computability-circular within the C2 chain): every step of the decomposition reduces to a known barrier; no polylog short-cut. **Verification log**: this synthesis is preceded by 19 verify slots (S167, S170-S189), with substantive empirical claim (21% fraction at canonical k_*) confirmed nineteen times by independent recomputation; PARTIAL verdicts in S176/S182/S184/S185/S186/S188/S189 all about scope qualifiers (rule-dependence of k_*, post-hoc precision pinning, finite-N convergence rate) not about the substantive claim. **Thread status**: DONE — recommendation: next commit slot pick Thread 2 (Connes-Consani-Moscovici operator amortisation per CLAUDE.md priority); proposed B-grade frame-shift follow-on for non-commit rotation: Voiculescu-Speicher higher free cumulants on the bulk to test Wigner-Dyson level-spacing prediction (NOT next commit thread). Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82, S148, S166, S168, S169. Cross-domain refs (compiled, all analytic NT — thread does NOT pass A-grade cross-domain criterion): Hardy-Wright Ch.16,18; Iwaniec-Kowalski §3.4; Davenport Ch.6,8,22; Tenenbaum §I.4.4-5; Wirsing 1956; Gallagher 1970; Montgomery-Vaughan Ch.9,17; Bai-Silverstein §3.3. See archive/sessions/session190_commit_s82_invariant_subspace.md. | 190 |
    /apps/aplikacijos/prime-research/status/CLOSED_PATHS.md:808:| S191 paradigm-shift session — Pointwise Ramanujan-spike approximator T_Q(n) := (π(N)/N) · Σ_{q sqf ≤ Q} mu(q) c_q(n)/phi(q): does the explicit pointwise dual of S168's energy-level spike formula exhibit the predicted 21% L²-fraction at Q ≈ N^{0.185} and does it function as a non-trivial primality predictor? | NEW CONSTRUCTION | C | Paradigm-shift session (no cross-domain imports allowed) — built the **pointwise** dual of S168's L²-only spike formula. **Hölder simplification (project's algebraic step)**: for squarefree q with d = gcd(q, n), `mu(q) c_q(n) / phi(q) = mu(d) / phi(q/d)`; verified numerically for all squarefree q ∈ [1, 30] and n ∈ [0, 60] in `sanity_holder.py`. **Object**: `T_Q(n) := (π(N)/N) · Σ_{q sqf ≤ Q} mu(gcd(q,n))/phi(q/gcd(q,n))`, computable in `O(Q · ω(n))` per n. **Empirical results** at d ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20}, Q ∈ {2, 6, N^{0.185}, N^{0.21}, 30, √N}: (a) **At d=20, Q=13≈N^{0.185}**: `||T_Q − const||² / π(N) = 0.2229`, matching S169's measured SVD spike-block fraction `0.220 ± 0.005` within **1.4%** — first pointwise empirical confirmation of S168/S169's energy-level prediction. (b) At d=14, 18 the construction overestimates SVD by 16-20%, exactly the "missing-spike" finite-d gap S169 disclosed. (c) **Primality-predictor lift over random baseline**: precision-at-π(N) = 0.183, 0.439, 0.524, 0.998 at Q = 2, 13, 30, 1024 (d=20), giving lifts 2.34×, 5.61×, 6.70×, 12.76× — monotonically increasing in Q. (d) **Wheel-Mertens identity** falls out of the closed form: at primorial Q=W, n coprime to W, `M_W(n) = ∏_{p|W}(1 + 1/(p−1)) = W/φ(W)`, hence `T_W(coprime n) = (π(N)/N) · W/φ(W)` — pointwise Mertens-density correction. **Edges composed**: E2.1 + E1.5 + E1.6 + E2.2 + S168. **Cross-domain technique imported: NONE** (paradigm-shift constraint satisfied). **Honest novelty**: pointwise form not in project's prior writeups (S166-S190 stayed at L² level); algebraic content is the Hölder simplification + wheel-Mertens identification + pointwise empirical S168-test. The Ramanujan-Fourier coefficients `mu(q)/phi(q)` of the von Mangoldt function are classical (Hardy 1921) — re-derived here from S168 alone per the no-imports constraint. **Closure mode C**: T_Q does not break C-circular (computing it requires π(N) for the prefactor). The 79% remainder ‖chi_P − T_Q‖² is the bulk MP component. **Falsification status**: all four pre-stated falsifiers PASS. **Lean candidate**: the Hölder identity `mu(q) c_q(n)/phi(q) = mu(gcd)/phi(q/gcd)` is one-step character-theoretic, suitable for L1 queue. See experiments/constructions/ramanujan_spike_pointwise/. | 191 |
